__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.gguf
result_*.json
!result_example.json
.env
*.log
# THE LOGS ARE SCRATCH, AND THE RULE IS THE DIRECTORY, NOT AN EXTENSION. `*.log` was the whole
# rule, so a capture written with any other extension was STAGEABLE -- `git add -A` could commit
# it, and it did: nine files under logs/ are tracked because of that gap, and scripts/run_guarded.sh
# still writes <workdir>/logs/<UNIT>.out. A single-file exception cannot be declared by an ignore
# rule, so the nine are declared in cabinet_root.py (LEGACY_CAPTURES) and the directory is closed.
logs/
# THE WORKTREES ARE THE BOARD'S, AND THE RULE IS THE DIRECTORY, NOT ITS CONTENTS. The kanban
# dispatcher creates <repo>/.worktrees/<task-id> BY DESIGN for project-linked worktree tasks, so
# the directory exists whenever such a card runs -- and it appears at the repository ROOT. With no
# rule, `git add -A` in main staged an EMBEDDED GIT REPOSITORY:
#     warning: adding embedded git repository: .worktrees/t_af6fafd9
#     add '.worktrees/t_af6fafd9/'
# -- a gitlink, which is exactly the class of accident CABINET_STRUCTURE.md rule 1 exists to make
# impossible ("A WORKTREE IS NOT TIDINESS ... the worktree makes that impossible rather than
# discouraged"). Naming the CONTENTS is not available: a running card puts whatever it likes in
# there, under a subject line nobody wrote about it. The DIRECTORY is what the repository can
# check -- `git check-ignore -v .worktrees/` -- which is why this is a directory rule, the same
# shape as the logs/ rule above and for the same reason.
.worktrees/
# AND THE DARTS HARNESS'S ARCHIVE DIRECTORY -- the same shape as the two directory rules above, and NOT
# a declaration that its contents are scratch. Its five tracked control bundles are DECLARED IN PLACE in
# cabinet_root.py (DARTS_ARCHIVE + DARTS_ARCHIVE_WHY), byte-frozen and cited by
# darts/DARTS_AMBIGUITIES.md. The rule closes the directory to NEW bytes so that a stray write beside
# them cannot be staged; `darts/darts.py`'s --out now resolves the DECLARED write scope's darts/ subtree
# instead of this directory, so nothing writes here at all. Read it back with
# `git check-ignore darts/results/x`. Tracked files stay tracked: an ignore rule does not untrack what
# is already committed.
darts/results/
.venv/
venv/
